/Title (When a distribution is symmetric about its center \(skewness = 0\), the mean, median, and mode coincide, and any of the three will do. Symmetrical, bell-shaped data is, however, the exception rather than the rule: house prices, personal income, social-media follower counts, daily stock returns, network traffic, and ShopCart order values are all skewed in practice. When a distribution is skewed, a long tail on one side pulls the mean toward that tail, while the median stays anchored near where most of the data actually sits - which is why the ordering of mean, median and mode is a quick visual diagnostic for the direction of skew \(mode less than median less than mean indicates a right/positive skew; the reverse indicates a left/negative skew\). )
/Dest [16 0 R /XYZ 72.327637 394.94189 0]
/Parent 4 0 R
/SE 64 0 R
/Prev 569 0 R
/Next 573 0 R
>>
endobj
