/Title (Quantiles are data points that split a distribution into equal-sized consecutive sets. The 2-quantile \(splitting the data into a lower and an upper half\) is exactly the median; the 4-quantiles are the quartiles \(three points splitting the data into four equal parts\); the 100-quantiles are the percentiles \(ninety-nine points splitting the data into a hundred equal parts\). )
/Dest [19 0 R /XYZ 72.531738 357.57227 0]
/Parent 4 0 R
/SE 89 0 R
/Prev 609 0 R
/Next 613 0 R
>>
endobj
612 0 obj
<<
/Title (Median and quartiles alone say nothing about the tails, so the five-number summary adds the two extremes: minimum, Q1, median, Q3, maximum. A boxplot is the standard visualization of this summary: the box spans from Q1 to Q3 \(so the box length is the IQR\), the median is drawn as a line inside the box, and whiskers extend out to the minimum and maximum observations \(or to 1.5 times IQR with individual outlier points marked beyond that, in the more common convention\). )
/Dest [20 0 R /XYZ 72.177246 167.81348 0]
/Parent 4 0 R
